---
// The tab convention, stated once, on every page that has tabs.
//
// Three ways, not two. Michal asked the question directly on 2 September —
// "Mám tam nějaký prompt? Jak to zpracuje to CLI?" — and Jordan had agreed the
// same mechanism on 5 August ("combined UI + API format"). A page that shows
// only the prompt and the clicks answers neither.
//
// TAB LABEL CONTRACT — these three strings, byte for byte, on every page:
//
//     Prompt        UI        CLI / API
//
// Starlight's <Tabs syncKey="howto"> remembers the reader's choice in
// localStorage under the *label text*, so a page that spells a label
// differently silently drops out of the sync and resets the reader.
//
// Plan mode, measured on 2026-09-08 (two staged runs in project 264): it adds
// one approval of a written plan and then asks for every change exactly as
// before — 8 approvals to the control run's 7. It is a review gate, not a way
// to approve once, and the wording below says so.
//
// Every task page carries three tabs as of 2026-09-15; `write/` keeps two, so
// both shapes exist. `manual={false}` is now an EXTRA paragraph rather than a
// branch that replaces the intro: on app/ it used to win over `tabs={3}` and
// hide the CLI tab from the reader entirely (guide test, 2026-09-15).
//
// The same wording in plain text lives in ./pathintro.mjs, because the markdown
// twin has to carry the "these are alternatives" sentence too — without it an
// agent reads the three flattened sections as three things to do in sequence.
//
// Usage:  <PathIntro tabs={3} />              — Prompt · UI · CLI / API
//         <PathIntro />                       — legacy two-tab wording
//         <PathIntro approvals={false} />     — read-only pages, where it does not
//         <PathIntro manual={false} />        — no click-by-click equivalent exists
//         <PathIntro yours="the Google consent screen" />
---

<div class="gs-pathintro">
  {
    Astro.props.tabs === 3 ? (
      <p>
        <strong>Three ways to do this, one page — they are alternatives, not steps.</strong>{' '}
        <strong>Prompt</strong> is what you paste into Kai; <strong>UI</strong> is the same task
        click by click, for when you want to see where each setting lives;{' '}
        <strong>CLI / API</strong> is the same task from a terminal, with the raw request beside
        it. Pick one tab and carry on — your choice follows you to the next page.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        <strong>Two ways to do this, one page — they are alternatives, not steps.</strong>{' '}
        <strong>Prompt</strong> is what you paste into Kai; <strong>UI</strong> is the same task
        click by click, for when you want to see where each setting lives. Either tab reaches the
        same result.
      </p>
    )
  }

  {
    Astro.props.manual === false && (
      <p>
        There is no click-by-click equivalent for building this: by hand it is a development
        workflow with its own section, and the <strong>UI</strong> tab says where to go for it.
      </p>
    )
  }

  {
    Astro.props.approvals !== false && (
      <p>
        Kai asks before it changes anything: expect one approval dialog per object it creates,
        showing the exact configuration it is about to write. Questions that only read do not ask.
        If confirming each one gets tiring, pre-approve tools in{' '}
        <a href="/kai/settings/#tool-permissions">tool permissions</a>. The chat's{' '}
        <strong>plan mode</strong> button is a different thing: Kai writes the steps down and asks
        you to approve the plan before it starts — and then still asks for each change. Use it when
        you want to read the plan first, not to save clicks.
      </p>
    )
  }

  {
    Astro.props.yours && (
      <p>
        One thing stays yours either way: {Astro.props.yours}.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <p class="gs-pathintro__recovery">
    If a step builds something other than what this page describes, say so in the
    same chat — it edits what it made. Nothing here is one-way.
  </p>
</div>
